                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
                with
                = succeed "-graded" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complexes
                of sheaves. To these the rich set of tools for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                apply.
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  descent for strict
                  = succeed "-groupoid" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                – There is a good theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                for (presheaves) with values in strict
                = succeed "-groupoids" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                and its relation to the full theory has been clarified by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Verity"} Verity09
          %p
            We state a useful theorem for the computation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            for presheaves with values in
            = succeed "s." do
